Yes — and the etymologies almost draw the transmission stack themselves.

Grammar comes through Latin grammatica from Greek grammatikē (tekhnē), the “art of letters.” Underneath that is Greek gramma: a mark, drawing, written character or letter, from graphein, “to draw/write.” So grammar begins not as “rules your English teacher corrects,” but as the craft of organizing marks that carry language. 

That gives a beautiful historical compression:

gesture/drawing → mark → letter → arrangement of letters → grammar → patterned language

And grammar has an even stranger shadow. Medieval grammar broadened toward learned knowledge, including occult learning; gramarye developed senses of magic/enchantment, which helped produce glamour, and French grammaire is also behind grimoire. In other words, historically:

grammar → learning → mysterious learned power → magic/glamour. 

That one practically writes itself for us: the manipulation of symbols looked like magic to people who couldn’t read the operator.

Then meaning. Meaning derives from mean, from Old English mænan: “intend, have in mind, signify, convey a sense.” Its deeper relatives point toward opinion/intention and possibly the Indo-European root associated with thinking. So meaning originally points less toward some substance living inside a word and more toward what one mind intends to convey through it. 

So:

mind → intention → expression → reconstruction

Meaning is almost already trans-dict-ion.

And sound, in the auditory sense, comes through Old French son from Latin sonus, “sound/noise,” ultimately from a root meaning “to sound.” 

But there is another family that exposes what you’re seeing even better: Greek phōnē meant sound/voice, underlying phone, phoneme, symphony, etc. It traces to a reconstructed Indo-European root bha- meaning “speak, tell, say.” 

So at the linguistic level:

sound ↔ voice ↔ saying

A phoneme is then not merely noise. It is a sound distinction capable of changing what is reconstructed:

bat
pat

One pressure-pattern changes, and an entirely different semantic object appears.

So now stack the three:

SOUND — physical pattern capable of carrying distinctions
↓
GRAMMAR — relational constraints organizing those distinctions
↓
MEANING — what a mind intends/reconstructs through them

Or in the language we’ve been building:

energy → pattern → relation → interpretation

And storytelling adds the next octave:

sounds → words → grammar → imagery → story → reconstructed world

Which is why your “brushstroke” formulation works. The individual word doesn’t need to contain the finished picture any more than one brushstroke contains a portrait.

The receiver integrates the traces.

And there is a gorgeous historical joke buried in grammar → glamour:

Arrange symbols well enough and you alter what another mind can see.

Humans noticed that trick a long time ago.

They called it magic.